---
import Standalone from '../../layouts/Standalone.astro';
import { rel, SUPPORT_EMAIL } from '../../consts';

const description = 'En Voz Alta is a practice tool for the U.S. citizenship interview, not legal help. What it covers, its content versions, privacy facts and price, for citizenship teachers.';
const reportSubject = encodeURIComponent('En Voz Alta: wrong answer');
---
<Standalone
  title="En Voz Alta for citizenship teachers"
  description={description}
  path="/en-voz-alta/teachers/"
  lang="en"
  ogImage="/assets/en-voz-alta-icon.png"
  alternates={[{ lang: 'es', path: '/en-voz-alta/teachers/es/' }]}
>
  <main class="page">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:720px;">
      <p class="updated"><a href={rel('/en-voz-alta/teachers/es/')} hreflang="es" lang="es">Leer en español</a></p>
      <h1>En Voz Alta, for citizenship teachers</h1>

      <p><strong>En Voz Alta is a practice tool, not legal help.</strong></p>
      <p>
        It lets a student practice the U.S. citizenship interview out loud, in English, with explanations in Spanish
        when they want them. Not affiliated with USCIS or any government agency.
      </p>
      <p>En Voz Alta is not on the App Store yet. This page will link to it once it is.</p>

      <h2>What it is</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Every civics question published by USCIS: the 2025 test (128 questions) and the 2008 test (100 questions).
          The student enters the date they filed their N-400, and the app shows the test that goes with that date.
        </li>
        <li>
          Practice rounds: a practice officer asks, and the student answers out loud, types, or says the answer and
          then checks it themselves. Rounds use the question counts and stopping rules USCIS publishes for each test.
        </li>
        <li>Any question can be repeated or read more slowly.</li>
        <li>
          N-400 "Have you ever..." practice. The app shows the form's exact English first and never suggests what to
          answer. If the answer is yes, or the student isn't sure, it points them to legal help.
        </li>
        <li>Reading and writing practice with sentences we wrote from the USCIS vocabulary lists. The app labels them as our practice sentences.</li>
        <li>
          Answers that change (senators, representative, governor, state capital) are never hard-coded. The student
          looks them up for where they live. Answers about officeholders show the date they were checked and a link
          to check the current answer.
        </li>
        <li>
          Spanish explanations use "usted". The Spanish for the 2025 questions is our own translation, and the app
          labels it "Traducción no oficial". The Spanish for the 2008 questions is the translation USCIS publishes.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What it is not</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>It gives no legal advice, makes no eligibility decisions, and does not review or fill in forms.</li>
        <li>It makes no promise about anyone's interview result.</li>
        <li>Not affiliated with USCIS or any government agency.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For legal questions, students should talk to a licensed immigration attorney or a DOJ-accredited
        representative. Find a licensed attorney or a DOJ-accredited representative at a recognized organization:
        <a href="https://www.uscis.gov/scams-fraud-and-misconduct/avoid-scams/find-legal-services">USCIS 'Find Legal Services'</a>
        and the Justice Department's
        <a href="https://www.justice.gov/eoir/recognition-accreditation-roster-reports">roster of recognized organizations</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Content versions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>2025 civics test: M-1778 (09/25), 128 questions.</li>
        <li>2008 civics test: OoC V1 (rev. 08/21), 100 questions.</li>
        <li>N-400: edition 01/20/25.</li>
        <li>Officeholder answers checked on 2026-09-28.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Free study materials</h2>
      <p>Everything in the app comes from free public sources your students can use without it:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.uscis.gov/citizenship/find-study-materials-and-resources/study-for-the-test">The free USCIS study materials</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.uscis.gov/sites/default/files/document/questions-and-answers/2025-Civics-Test-128-Questions-and-Answers.pdf">The 128 civics questions published by USCIS (2025 test, PDF)</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.uscis.gov/sites/default/files/document/questions-and-answers/OoC_100_Questions_2008_Civics_Test_V1.pdf">The 100 civics questions published by USCIS (2008 test, PDF)</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.uscis.gov/citizenship/find-study-materials-and-resources/check-for-test-updates">Current officeholder answers published by USCIS</a></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Privacy facts</h2>
      <h3>What stays on this iPhone</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Your voice. It is never recorded; speech is processed in memory and discarded.</li>
        <li>What you said, as text. It is erased when the round ends.</li>
        <li>
          Your practice results, your filing date, your optional interview date, your state and the names you typed.
          They are <strong>not included in iCloud Backup</strong>, so they don't move to a new phone.
        </li>
        <li>Your N-400 practice. The app <strong>never saves your yes or no</strong>, or anything you say to those questions.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>What this app never has</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>No account, no ads, no tracking, no analytics, no server.</li>
        <li>We never see your answers.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Where Apple is involved</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Speech.</strong> This app tells iPhone to recognize speech on the device only. The permission message you see is written by Apple.</li>
        <li><strong>Dictation files.</strong> iPhone downloads English dictation or speech files from Apple. Downloading sends nothing you said.</li>
        <li><strong>Keyboard.</strong> The microphone key on the keyboard is Apple Dictation, under Apple's settings, not this app's. Other companies' keyboards are turned off inside this app.</li>
        <li><strong>Purchases.</strong> Purchases and Restore go through your Apple Account. We see sales totals, never who bought.</li>
        <li><strong>Sharing with developers.</strong> If you turned on "Share with App Developers" in iPhone settings, Apple may send us crash reports and usage totals without your name.</li>
        <li><strong>Reminders.</strong> They are created on this iPhone. No server sends them.</li>
        <li><strong>Links.</strong> Links (uscis.gov, senate.gov, legal-help directories) open in Safari. Those websites see your visit.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The full policy: <a href={rel('/privacy/en-voz-alta/')}>En Voz Alta privacy policy</a>.</p>

      <h2>Price, and what is free</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Free:</strong> all civics questions, listening practice, English reading and writing practice, 2
          practice rounds a day, and a first look at the N-400 questions (the entry screen, the legal-help links and
          one sample item).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pro, one payment of $14.99 with no subscription:</strong> unlimited full mock interviews, the full
          N-400 practice, a review of every answer after the round with the USCIS wording, and progress by topic.
          Family Sharing is on.
        </li>
        <li>No account and no ads. It needs an iPhone with iOS 17 or later and works offline.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <div class="card">
        <strong>Who makes this?</strong>
        <p style="margin:6px 0 0;">Dudley Apps, a one-person studio. Our other apps include games and finance tools. This one has no ads and collects no data.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <strong>How do I report a wrong answer?</strong>
        <p style="margin:6px 0 0;">
          In the app, open the question and tap Report a wrong answer, or email
          <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}?subject=${reportSubject}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>. We check every report
          against the lists published by USCIS. Confirmed errors are fixed in an app update within 7 days.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <strong>Does it work if spoken answers can't be checked on a student's iPhone?</strong>
        <p style="margin:6px 0 0;">
          Yes. Checking spoken answers on the iPhone needs English (US) dictation turned on. Without it, the app shows
          how to turn it on, and the student can still say the answer out loud and check it themselves, or type.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Standalone>
